= Daikon (大根 / 蘿蔔 / मूलक)

_The long white East Asian winter radish, *Raphanus sativus* L. var. *longipinnatus*, Brassicaceae. *This entry is unusual in this book for being classical in all four systems* — a canonical Bencao drug (萊菔), a saṃhitā vegetable-and-drug (मूलक mūlaka), an Avicennan simple (فجل fijl), and a Dioscoridean one (ῥαφανίς) — so almost nothing here is reasoned extension, and the sections cite rather than apply. Two referent points govern it. First, *the classical traditions treat the species, not the cultivar*: no canon distinguishes the East Asian long white form as a separate drug, so "radish" in Dioscorides and Avicenna is the Mediterranean form and *not* daikon, and the entry says so where it matters. *A corrective worth making, though:* the classical Mediterranean radish, as Theophrastus and Pliny describe its varieties, was *larger and longer* than the modern small red globe radish, and in form was closer to daikon than to what a European reader now pictures. Second, *this is a multiple-drug-form substance in the manner of ginger and rehmannia* — the Chinese tradition treats raw and cooked root as having *opposite* directional actions, treats the seed (萊菔子) as a distinct and more important drug than the root, and gives the spent bolted root its own name; Ayurveda splits the drug by *maturity* rather than by cooking. The drug-form structure is the entry's spine._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* *_Raphanus sativus_ L.* var. *_longipinnatus_* L. H. Bailey, family *Brassicaceae* (Cruciferae). Drug names: *Radix Raphani* (root), and for the canonical Chinese drug *Semen Raphani* (萊菔子, the seed). *_Raphanus_* ← Greek ῥάφανος — *and the borrowing carries a lexicographic trap*, below. *_sativus_* = "sown, cultivated" (Lewis & Short).

*Greek:* *ῥαφανίς (rhaphanís)* — *radish*, and the word Dioscorides uses. *ῥάφανος (rháphanos)* — *in Attic usage, cabbage*, not radish; the two words are near-identical and name different plants, and *Liddell & Scott records both senses*, with the dialectal distribution varying. *A translator meeting ῥάφανος in an Attic source is probably reading about cabbage*, which is a live hazard in handling Greek dietetic texts. The traditional etymology derives the word from ῥᾷ (_rhâi_, "easily") + φαίνεσθαι (_phaínesthai_, "to appear") — "the quickly-appearing one," on the radish's famously rapid germination. *This is a folk etymology and should be labelled one*: modern Greek etymology treats ῥάφανος / ῥαφανίς as most likely *Pre-Greek*, i.e. a substrate word of non-Indo-European origin, with the ῥᾷ-φαίνεσθαι analysis an ancient rationalization.#footnote[_Liddell & Scott_, s.v. ῥαφανίς and ῥάφανος — the radish/cabbage distinction and its dialectal variation. The ῥᾷ + φαίνεσθαι derivation is the ancient etymology and is repeated in older lexicography; Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_, treats the word as Pre-Greek, which is the current view. Flagged as folk etymology per this project's convention. Lewis & Short, s.v. _raphanus_, _sativus_.]

*English:* *_Daikon_* ← Japanese 大根 (below); in English from the mid-20th century, with Japanese cuisine. *_Mooli_* ← Hindi मूली (_mūlī_) is the usual British term for the same vegetable, so *British "mooli" and American "daikon" are the same thing under names borrowed from opposite ends of Asia*. Also *white radish*, *winter radish*, *Chinese radish*, *Japanese radish*, *Oriental radish*.

*And "radish" itself means "root"* — Old English _rædic_ ← Latin *_radix_* ("root"), per the OED. *Which makes a four-way convergence worth pausing on:* English _radish_ ← _radix_ "root"; Japanese 大根 "big root"; Sanskrit मूलक ← मूल "root"; Hindi मूली from the same. *Four traditions independently named this plant after the fact that it is a root* — a convergence that says something about how the vegetable presents itself and nothing at all about shared transmission.

*Chinese:* *蘿蔔 / 萝卜 (luóbo)* — the ordinary modern name. *萊菔 (láifú)* — *the classical Bencao drug-name*, and the form under which the pharmacological literature treats it; the _Erya_ (爾雅) records the older *葖 (tū)* and *蘆萉 (lúfěi)*. The root enters the _Mingyi Bielu_ (名醫別錄) and is treated at length by Li Shizhen in the _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目, 1578), 菜部.

*A naming trap, and this book has already met its mirror image.* *胡蘿蔔 (húluóbo)* — "foreign radish" — *is the carrot*, not a radish: the 胡 prefix is the canonical Chinese marker of a Silk-Road introduction, as `substances/sesame-seeds.typ` records for 胡麻 (sesame) and 胡椒 (black pepper). *So 蘿蔔 is daikon and 胡蘿蔔 is carrot, and the second is not a kind of the first.* The mirror case is Japanese: 人参 (_ninjin_) now means *carrot* in ordinary Japanese, the word having been transferred from ginseng to the European root on a shape resemblance, so that ginseng requires the qualifier 朝鮮人参 — see `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`. *Two East Asian languages renamed the carrot after a more famous root, in opposite directions*, and both renamings trap readers of recipes and of materia medica alike.

*Japanese:* *大根 (daikon)* — 大 (_dai_, "big") + 根 (_kon_, "root"), the Sino-Japanese reading; the native Japanese reading of the same characters is *おおね (ōne)*, the older form. *すずしろ (suzushiro)* — the archaic and poetic name, and the one that matters classically: *suzushiro is one of the 春の七草 (haru no nanakusa, the Seven Herbs of Spring)*, eaten as 七草粥 (_nanakusa-gayu_, seven-herb rice porridge) on the seventh day of the first month — a seasonal-dietetic observance with a documented Heian-period court history.

*Sanskrit:* *मूलक (mūlaka)* — from मूल (_mūla_, "root") + the suffix -क (_-ka_); Monier-Williams. Fully classical: the drug appears in Caraka's and Suśruta's *śāka-varga* (the vegetable class of the dietetic chapters) and throughout the later nighaṇṭus. *The classical texts name its two forms separately* — बाल मूलक (_bāla mūlaka_, the tender young root) and वृद्ध / महा मूलक (_vṛddha / mahā mūlaka_, the mature large root) — and assign them *opposite* properties; see the Ayurvedic section, where this is the main event. Hindi मूली (_mūlī_); Bengali মুলা (_mulā_).

*Arabic:* *فجل (fijl)* — radish, fully canonical in Tibb; *Persian* ترب (_torob_) and فجل; *Urdu / Hakeemi* مولی (_mūlī_), the Hindi-derived form used in South Asian Unani. The seed is *بزر الفجل (bazr al-fijl)*. *Horseradish* is distinguished as *فجل حار (fijl ḥārr,* "hot radish") — the term `substances/garlic.typ` uses in its *badal* list.

*Korean:* 무 (_mu_); the older 무우 (_muu_). Relevant below for 깍두기 and 동치미.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Fully classical. 萊菔 enters the_ Mingyi Bielu _and is treated substantially in the_ Bencao Gangmu _(1578), 菜部; the seed 萊菔子 is a standard drug of the modern pharmacopoeia in the 消食藥 (food-dispersing) category. The root is primarily a_ dietetic _drug and the seed primarily a_ medicinal _one — a split worth keeping in view, because most of the formal pharmacology attaches to the seed._

*The root (蘿蔔 / 萊菔) — and raw and cooked are classically opposite drugs.* The canonical statement, which the _Bencao Gangmu_ transmits, is *生食升氣，熟食降氣* — "eaten raw it raises qi; eaten cooked it descends qi." This is the clearest instance in the vegetable materia medica of the substance-by-processing principle this book treats for ginger and rehmannia.

- *生蘿蔔 (raw):* *Property* — cool (涼). *Flavor* — acrid/pungent (辛), sweet (甘). *Channels* — Lung (肺), Stomach (胃). *Actions* — *清熱生津* (clears heat and generates fluids), *化痰* (transforms phlegm), *涼血止血* (cools the blood and stops bleeding), *下氣寬中* weakly. The register is heat, thirst, thick phlegm, and the hoarse or sore throat.
- *熟蘿蔔 (cooked):* *Property* — neutral (平), some sources slightly warm (微溫). *Flavor* — sweet (甘), the acrid edge cooked off. *Channels* — Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Actions* — *下氣消食* (descends qi and disperses food stagnation), *健脾* (strengthens the Spleen), *寬中* (relaxes the middle), *利尿*. The register is fullness, distension, food stagnation, and heavy eating.

*The seed (萊菔子, láifúzǐ, Semen Raphani) — the canonical drug.* *Property* — neutral (平). *Flavor* — acrid (辛), sweet (甘). *Channels* — Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions* — *消食除脹* (disperses food stagnation and eliminates distension) and *降氣化痰* (descends qi and transforms phlegm). It is one of the standard drugs of the *消食* category and appears in 保和丸 and 三子養親湯 (below). *Drug-forms:* *炒萊菔子* (stir-fried) is the usual form, and is the one for 消食降氣; *生萊菔子* (raw) is more dispersing and was classically used to induce vomiting, which is not a modern indication.

*Minor drug-forms, recorded because the tradition names them:*
- *萊菔葉 / 蘿蔔纓 (leaves, tops)* — acrid, bitter, neutral; *消食理氣*, *化痰*. Nutritionally the richest part of the plant and the part usually discarded (see Modern).
- *地骷髏 (dìkūlóu,* "earth skeleton"), also *仙人骨 (xiānrén gǔ,* "immortal's bone") — *the dried, spent, woody root left in the ground after the plant has bolted and set seed*. A minor drug for *利水消腫* (promotes urination, reduces swelling) and *消食*. The names are worth noting for what they record: a root that has given everything to the seed-stalk and is left hollow and fibrous, which is a fair description of the material.

*Cautions:* the root is cool and *下氣*, so it is cautioned in *氣虛* (qi vacuity) and *脾胃虛寒* (Spleen-Stomach vacuity cold), especially raw. *The seed carries the sharper caution precisely because it works better*: 萊菔子 is a dispersing-and-descending drug, and *氣虛者慎用* — use cautiously in qi vacuity — is the standard formulation, since it disperses what a vacuity patient needs to keep. *And this is the mechanism behind the famous folk incompatibility with ginseng*, taken up under Viruddha.#footnote[萊菔 in the _Mingyi Bielu_ (名醫別錄) and Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 菜部 — the 生食升氣，熟食降氣 formulation is transmitted there and is standard in the later dietetic literature; the exact wording should be verified against a specific edition. 萊菔子 properties, channels, actions, the 炒 / 生 drug-form distinction and the 氣虛者慎用 caution: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press), 消食藥 category; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition), Semen Raphani. 地骷髏 and 蘿蔔纓 are minor drugs recorded in the Bencao and dietetic literature.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Fully classical. मूलक (mūlaka) appears in the_ śāka-varga _of Caraka's and Suśruta's dietetic chapters and throughout the nighaṇṭu literature. *The classical treatment is unusual and is the reason this section is substantive rather than a formality*: the saṃhitās split the drug by_ maturity _and assign the two forms opposite doṣic effects — a drug-form distinction on a different axis from the Chinese raw/cooked split, and arriving at the same insight that the substance is not one thing._

*The maturity split, which is the classical point.*
- *बाल मूलक (bāla mūlaka,* the tender young root): *laghu* (लघु, light), *dīpana* (digestive-kindling), easily digested, and — the striking claim — *tridoṣa-śamana*, pacifying *all three* doṣas. Very few items in the canon carry a tridoṣa-pacifying attribution, and a young radish is an unexpected one.
- *वृद्ध / महा मूलक (vṛddha / mahā mūlaka,* the mature large root): *guru* (गुरु, heavy), *abhiṣyandi* (अभिष्यन्दि — channel-clogging, secretion-obstructing), difficult to digest, and *tridoṣa-prakopa*, aggravating all three. *The inversion is total*, and it is stated in the texts rather than inferred.

*This is not a prabhāva and should not be dressed as one*: the inversion is a *guṇa*-level fact — the root becomes heavy, fibrous and pungent-acrid as it matures — and follows from the standard parameters once maturity is admitted as a variable.

*Rasa:* *Kaṭu* (कटु, pungent) predominant, with *Tikta* (तिक्त, bitter); the mature root more sharply kaṭu.

*Guṇa:* *Laghu* (लघु) and *Rūkṣa* (रूक्ष, dry) with *Tīkṣṇa* (तीक्ष्ण, sharp) for *bāla mūlaka*; *Guru* (गुरु) and *Abhiṣyandi* (अभिष्यन्दि) for *vṛddha mūlaka*.

*Vīrya:* *Uṣṇa* (उष्ण, heating) — *and note the cross-system divergence, which should not be smoothed*: Ayurveda and Tibb both read the radish as *heating*, while 中醫 reads the raw root as *cool* (涼) and the cooked as neutral. *The systems genuinely disagree about this substance's thermal nature.* The disagreement is tractable rather than arbitrary — the Indian and Greco-Arabic readings weight the *pungent-acrid, wind-generating, irritant* character, while the Chinese reading weights the *fluid-generating, thirst-allaying, phlegm-cooling* effect of the watery raw root — but it is a real divergence and the entry records it as one.

*Vipāka:* *Kaṭu* (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* *None assigned.* Per this project's prabhāva rule the explicit statement is required: *the saṃhitās and nighaṇṭus assign mūlaka no distinct prabhāva*, and the drug's actions — digestive, carminative, Kapha-reducing, diuretic, voice-clearing — follow predictably from kaṭu rasa, uṣṇa vīrya, tīkṣṇa guṇa and kaṭu vipāka. *The maturity inversion, which is the most interesting thing about the classical treatment, is a guṇa-level phenomenon and not a prabhāva-level one*, and calling it one would misuse the category.

*Doṣa effect:* for *bāla mūlaka*, *tridoṣa-śamana* per the classical attribution. For *vṛddha mūlaka*, *tridoṣa-prakopa*. Reading the parameters directly: *Kapha-reducing* (kaṭu-uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa — the clearest and least disputed effect, and the one all three Asian systems agree on); *Pitta-aggravating* in quantity (uṣṇa vīrya, kaṭu rasa and vipāka); *Vāta* — *this one is genuinely two-sided*, since the drug is *vāta-anulomana* (wind-conducting, carminative) in its digestive action yet *rūkṣa-laghu* and classically *wind-generating* in quantity, which is the same tension the Greco-Arabic sources record as the radish's flatulence.

*Karma:* *Dīpana*, *pācana* (digestive-kindling and -ripening), *rocana / rucya* (appetite-restoring), *kaphahara*, *vāta-anulomana*, *śvāsa-hara* and *kāsa-hara* (relieving dyspnoea and cough — the seed and the juice especially), *svarya* (voice-benefiting, for hoarseness — a classical and specific indication), *mūtrala* (diuretic, with the stone-dissolving use shared with Tibb), *bhedana* (mildly purgative), *arśoghna* (benefiting haemorrhoids), *kṛmighna* (anthelmintic). In excess: *pitta-prakopa*, *raktapitta* (bleeding disorders — the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa caution), *vāta-prakopa* via the rūkṣa quality, and *āma* formation from *vṛddha mūlaka*'s abhiṣyandi heaviness.#footnote[मूलक in the _śāka-varga_ of Caraka Sūtrasthāna 27 (annapāna-vidhi) and Suśruta Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, śāka-varga, for the later elaboration. The bāla / vṛddha mūlaka distinction with opposed tridoṣa effects is classical and is among the more frequently cited drug-form distinctions in the vegetable class; the specific wording and the degree of emphasis vary between the saṃhitās and the nighaṇṭus and should be verified against a specific edition. Monier-Williams for मूलक and अभिष्यन्दि. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, for the modern consolidated treatment. The thermal divergence from 中醫 is stated rather than resolved, per this project's standard on cross-system disagreement.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

*This is one of the better-attested viruddha sections in the book, because mūlaka is a named member of Caraka's own incompatibility list rather than a substance incompatibilities had to be inferred for.*

- *(S) Mūlaka with milk (दुग्ध, dugdha) — the principal classical incompatibility, and a named saṃyoga-viruddha.* Caraka Sūtrasthāna 26 lists radish among the items not to be combined with milk, with *kuṣṭha* (the skin-disease class) given as the consequence of habitual violation. *`substances/milk.typ` already records this at (S) tier from the milk side*, and this entry matches that attribution rather than restating or re-rating it. *The classical reasoning is coherent on its own terms:* milk is *madhura-śīta-snigdha-guru* and mūlaka is *kaṭu-uṣṇa-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa*, a direct opposition of every parameter, and the saṃhitās treat such opposition as producing *āma* that lodges in *rasa* and *rakta* and surfaces in the skin.
- *(S) Mūlaka with honey — recorded with its qualifier.* `substances/honey.typ` lists radish among honey's saṃyoga-viruddha at (S) tier, *marked "some sources"*, and this entry carries the same rating and the same hedge. *And here there is a genuine cross-system divergence that should be stated rather than smoothed:* the Japanese folk cough remedy *大根飴 (daikon-ame)* — grated or cubed daikon steeped in honey, the syrup drunk for cough and sore throat — *is precisely this combination*, and it is among the most widely used household remedies in Japan. *So a combination that one tradition names as an incompatibility, another uses as a standard remedy.* Neither reading is obviously wrong: the Ayurvedic objection is to habitual dietary combination in quantity, the Japanese use is a short-course medicinal preparation for an acute complaint, and the categories may simply not be commensurable. *The divergence is flagged and left open.*
- *(N) Later-nighaṇṭu and regional additions:* radish with jaggery, and radish with certain fish, appear in later and regional incompatibility lists; *the attestation is weaker than for the milk pairing and the sources disagree*, so they are recorded as (N)-and-below rather than asserted.
- *(S / conditional) The conditional categories, which the texts specify directly for this drug:*
  - *Avasthā- and mātrā-viruddha* — *vṛddha mūlaka in quantity*: the mature root is *guru* and *abhiṣyandi* and is classically unsuitable for the weak-digestioned and the convalescent. The maturity distinction *is itself* a conditional-incompatibility rule, not merely a quality grading.
  - *Doṣa-viruddha* — mūlaka in *raktapitta* (bleeding disorders) and in frank Pitta excess, on the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa grounds.
- *(F) Folk — the Chinese ginseng prohibition, and it stays at folk tier.* 蘿蔔 is traditionally not taken with *人參* (ginseng), the radish being held to *disperse the qi* that the ginseng supplements. *`substances/korean-ginseng.typ` and `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ` both record this at (F) tier, explicitly as "folk-dietetic rather than textual," and this entry does not upgrade it.* *What is worth adding is that the folk rule is internally coherent in a way many are not:* 萊菔子 is a formal *消食 / 降氣* drug — dispersing and descending — and 人參 is the archetypal *補氣* drug, so the pairing is a straightforward 補-versus-瀉 opposition in the system's own terms. *That coherence is not the same as textual attestation*, and the specific claim that radish "destroys" or "neutralizes" ginseng is not a Bencao statement. Widely observed in Chinese and Korean households.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Goitrogens.* Like other Brassicaceae, daikon carries *glucosinolates* whose breakdown products — notably *goitrin* — can inhibit thyroid iodine organification. *Clinically relevant only at high habitual raw intake combined with iodine deficiency*; cooking inactivates much of the pathway. Relevant to iodine-deficient populations and to existing hypothyroidism; not a general caution.
  - *Wind and bloating.* The classical flatulence attribution has a modern correlate in the fermentable fibre and the sulphur compounds; raw daikon in quantity is a plausible aggravator in irritable bowel syndrome. *The traditional correctives converge*: salt and vinegar in the Mediterranean, *hiṅgu* (asafoetida) and *ajwain* in India (see `substances/asafoetida.typ`), and taking it *with or after food* rather than before in both Tibb and Greco-Roman practice.
  - *萊菔子 as a medicinal, not a food*: at therapeutic dose it is a dispersing-descending drug and is cautioned in qi vacuity, in pregnancy, and alongside qi-tonifying prescriptions — the formal counterpart of the folk rule above.
  - *Thyroid medication and anticoagulants*: no established interaction for the food; the goitrogen point above is the only thyroid-relevant one, and it concerns iodine status rather than drug metabolism.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Fully canonical. فجل (fijl) is a simple drug of Ibn Sīnā's_ al-Qānūn _Book II and of Ibn al-Bayṭār's_ al-Jāmiʿ, _with a continuous presence in South Asian Unani under the Hindi-derived name مولی (mūlī). *The referent caveat applies*: the classical Arabic فجل is the Mediterranean and Persian radish, not the East Asian daikon — though as noted at the top, the classical form was longer and larger than the modern European globe radish. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the humoral framework._

*Names:* Arabic فجل (_fijl_); the seed بزر الفجل (_bazr al-fijl_); Persian ترب (_torob_); Urdu / Hakeemi مولی (_mūlī_). Horseradish is distinguished as فجل حار (_fijl ḥārr_).

*Mizāj (temperament):* *Hot and Dry, 2nd–3rd degree* — with the *leaf and seed hotter and drier than the root*, and the seed generally placed at the 3rd degree. *This converges with the Ayurvedic uṣṇa reading and diverges from the Chinese cool-raw-root reading*, as the Ayurvedic section notes.

*Afʿāl (actions):* *jālī* (جالي, detergent / cleansing), *mufattiḥ* (مفتح, deobstruent — *specifically for liver and spleen obstructions*, which is the drug's principal classical register and the one with a living modern survival), *mudirr al-bawl* (مدر البول, diuretic, *with the lithotriptic use for حصى / stone*), *hāḍim* (هاضم, digestive), *muqaṭṭiʿ al-balgham* (مقطع البلغم, phlegm-cutting), *muqawwī al-miʿda* (stomach-strengthening — *but see the caution, which is unusually pointed*), and *kāsir al-rīḥ* weakly.

*A canonical caution that is also a canonical instruction, and it is specific enough to be worth quoting in substance.* The Greco-Arabic sources are *not* uniformly favourable to the radish: Dioscorides and the Arabic authors following him record it as *flatulent, bad for the stomach, and provoking belching* — *muwallid li-l-riyāḥ* (مولد للرياح, wind-generating). *The classical resolution is a rule of order:* taken *before* food the radish causes eructation and disturbs the stomach, while taken *after* food it assists digestion and carries the meal downward. *So the same drug is harmful or useful depending on its position in the meal* — one of the more precise dietetic instructions in the classical corpus, and an instance of *krama* (sequence) functioning as a pharmacological variable in the Greco-Arabic tradition just as it does in the Ayurvedic *krama-viruddha* category.

*Istemālāt (indications):* حصى الكلى والمثانة (_ḥaṣā al-kulā wa-l-mathāna_, kidney and bladder stones) — the signature indication; يرقان (_yaraqān_, jaundice) and liver-and-spleen obstruction; *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic diseases); سعال (_suʿāl_, cough — the seed and the syrup); weak digestion and post-prandial heaviness.

*Mawāniʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* *ṣafrāwī* (bilious) and hot-dry temperaments; gastric weakness, gastritis and ulcer — the wind-generating and irritant character; and the before-food position described above.

*Muṣliḥ (corrective adjuvant):* *salt*, *vinegar*, and *olive oil* — the classical correctives, and the ones Mediterranean eating still applies to a raw radish without thinking about it; also *honey* in some sources, and *taking it after food* as the positional corrective. *That the everyday Egyptian and Levantine way of eating radishes — raw, with salt, alongside bread and a meal — is precisely the classically corrected form is a convergence worth marking*, and an instance of a dietetic rule surviving as table custom after the theory behind it has been forgotten.

*Badal (substitute):* *فجل حار (fijl ḥārr,* horseradish) for the strongly-pungent function, as `substances/garlic.typ` records; *خردل (khardal,* mustard) for the same register; *كرفس (karafs,* celery seed) where the diuretic-lithotriptic action is wanted rather than the pungency.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* *No attestation, and the nearest list pointedly omits it.* Radish does not appear in the Qur'ān or in the canonical hadith collections as a medicinal or dietary item. *The nearest thing is an absence worth noting:* the Qur'ānic vegetable list at 2:61 — in which the Israelites of the wilderness demand the foods of Egypt — names بقل (_baql_, herbs), قثاء (_qiththāʾ_, cucumber), فوم (_fūm_, garlic — see `substances/garlic.typ`), عدس (_ʿadas_, lentils) and بصل (_baṣal_, onion), *but not the radish*, despite the radish being a well-attested staple of pharaonic Egypt (below). *Recorded only to close off the inference*; nothing should be built on a silence.#footnote[فجل in Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (_al-Adwiya al-Mufrada_), and Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ li-Mufradāt al-Adwiya_; the lithotriptic and deobstruent indications and the Hot-Dry 2nd–3rd degree classification are the standard attributions, with the usual variation between sources on the exact degree and on root-versus-seed. The before-food/after-food rule originates with Dioscorides (_De materia medica_ II, ῥαφανίς) and is transmitted through the Arabic authors; the substance of the instruction is well attested, and the exact wording should be verified against a specific edition rather than taken from this entry. Modern Unani: _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_ and the AYUSH _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_ for the مولی entry. Qur'ān 2:61 vegetable list per the standard exegetical identifications, as given in `substances/garlic.typ`.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*萊菔子 is a formula drug; the root is a dietetic one.* The two named formulas that matter:

- *保和丸 (Bǎo Hé Wán,* "Preserve Harmony Pill") — the standard *food-stagnation* formula, in which 萊菔子 supplies the *消食除脹* and *降氣* action alongside 山楂 (hawthorn, for meat-and-fat stagnation), 神麯 (medicated leaven), 陳皮, 半夏, 茯苓 and 連翹. *This book's formula entries cite 保和丸 repeatedly as the prescription wanted when food stagnation is mistaken for Spleen vacuity* — see `formulas/six-gentlemen-decoction-with-aucklandia-and-amomum.typ` and the adjacent-formulas gloss in `zz-glossary.typ`.
- *三子養親湯 (Sān Zǐ Yǎng Qīn Tāng,* "Three-Seed Decoction to Nourish One's Parents") — 萊菔子 + 白芥子 + 紫蘇子, for *cough and wheezing with copious phlegm and qi counterflow in the elderly*. *The name is a Confucian filial conceit* of the same family as 四君子湯's: three seeds to care for aged parents, the indication being the phlegm-and-wheeze of old age. A small, elegant, three-drug formula and a good illustration of 降氣化痰 as a formula strategy.
- *萊菔子 in the food-stagnation quartet* with 麥芽, 山楂 and 神麯 — recorded in `substances/barley.typ`, which also gives the related charred trio *焦三仙*.

*Dietetic preparations of the root:*
- *蘿蔔湯 / 蘿蔔絲湯* — simmered radish soup; the canonical *cooked, descending, 消食* form, eaten after heavy meals and through the winter.
- *蘿蔔排骨湯* — with pork rib; the household winter form.
- *白蘿蔔蜂蜜水* — grated radish with honey or rock sugar for cough and hoarseness, the Chinese counterpart of the Japanese 大根飴, and subject to the same flagged divergence with the Ayurvedic honey-radish viruddha.
- *醃蘿蔔 / 蘿蔔乾* — salted and dried forms, the storage preparations.
- *地骷髏* is decocted as a minor drug, not eaten.

=== Ayurveda

*Mūlaka is treated as a śāka (vegetable) with medicinal use rather than as a formula drug*, and it is not a major ingredient of the classical compound formulary. Its classical place is in the *dietetic* chapters and in simple preparations:

- *Mūlaka-svarasa* (fresh root juice) — the classical form for the diuretic, lithotriptic and *śvāsa-kāsa* indications; the use overlaps substantially with the Tibb lithotriptic register, which is a genuine convergence between two traditions that shared Persianate transmission.
- *Mūlaka as pathya* (prescribed diet) in *kapha* disorders, *gulma*, *arśas* (haemorrhoids) and *kṛmi* (worms); as *apathya* in *raktapitta* and in Pitta disorders.
- *Mūlī sabzi* and *mūlī paratha* — the living North Indian preparations, *and note that both are conventionally made with hiṅgu (asafoetida) and ajwain*, the carminative correctives for exactly the wind-generating property the classical texts attribute to the drug. See `substances/asafoetida.typ`. *The folk kitchen applies the classical correction without citing it.*
- *Mūlaka leaves* are eaten as a separate śāka, and the classical texts treat the leaf as lighter than the mature root.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*Fully classical, and the radish had a standing in antiquity disproportionate to its modern status as a salad garnish.*

- *Dioscorides, _De materia medica_ II* (ῥαφανίς) — the foundational entry: diuretic, flatulent, bad for the stomach, provoking belching, better eaten after food; the seed used medicinally; the leaf also eaten.
- *Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_ VII* — the varieties of radish, with the distinctions of size and form that establish the classical radish as a larger plant than the modern European globe type.
- *Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XIX–XX* — an extended treatment; Pliny reports that the physician *Moschion* devoted an entire volume to the radish, and records the tradition of a *golden radish dedicated at Delphi* alongside a beet of silver and a turnip of lead. *The Delphic dedication and the Moschion monograph are Plinian reports and are flagged as such* — they tell us about the radish's standing in the Greek dietetic imagination rather than about its pharmacology, and the exact book-and-chapter references should be verified against a specific edition.
- *Galen* treats the radish in the dietetic works as producing a coarse or bad humour while being useful as a deobstruent and diuretic — the ambivalence the Arabic authors inherit and systematize into the before-and-after-food rule.
- *Pharaonic Egypt* — the radish is well attested as an Egyptian staple, and Herodotus (II.125) reports radishes, onions and garlic as the provisions of the pyramid workers. *The Herodotean passage is a classical report of uncertain reliability and is cited as such*, but the radish's presence in Egyptian agriculture is independently attested, and radish-seed oil was pressed there.

*Modern Western survival — and there is a real one, which most of the classical materia medica cannot claim.* *Black radish (_Raphanus sativus_ var. _niger_) juice* remains in use in French and German phytotherapy as a *choleretic and hepatobiliary remedy* — _jus de radis noir_, _Schwarzer Rettich_ — for dyspepsia and sluggish bile, sold as a pressed juice or syrup. *This is a direct and continuous survival of the Avicennan mufattiḥ liver-and-spleen indication*, which is worth marking because such survivals are rare: the indication, the organ and the plant all match across nine centuries. The clinical evidence is modest and the preparation is a traditional-use registration rather than an evidence-based one. The culinary radish, meanwhile, has been reduced in Western eating to a raw garnish — *the one classical form the sources specifically caution against taking before a meal.*

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

- *Simple preparations dominate*: the pressed juice (عصير الفجل) for stone and jaundice; the seed (بزر الفجل) decocted or powdered for cough and as a stronger diuretic; the root eaten raw *with salt and vinegar* as a corrected dietetic item.
- *سفوف (sufūf)* — powder preparations incorporating the seed for the diuretic-lithotriptic indication.
- *Living table custom*: raw radish with salt, bread and a meal remains standard across Egypt, the Levant, Iran and South Asia, and — as noted above — is the classically corrected form of consumption.
- *The South Asian Unani مولی entry* carries the same indications under the Hindi-derived name, with the North Indian *mūlī* preparations overlapping the Ayurvedic ones; the two systems have shared this drug and its correctives for centuries.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Daikon is a *cool-season, autumn-sown, winter-harvested* crop, and its traditional forms are storage forms — which makes the climate section unusually tightly coupled to the agronomy. Three zones carry the traditional use. See_ climates.typ _for the canonical frameworks._

*Temperate and continental-monsoon East Asia — the core zone, and the home of the maxim that explains the whole substance.* Northern and central China, Korea and Japan: hot humid summers and cold dry winters, with a genuine hungry season when no fresh vegetable grows. Daikon is sown late summer, harvested late autumn, and *stored* — and the traditional preparations are storage technologies: *切り干し大根 (kiriboshi daikon,* sun-dried shredded), *沢庵 (takuan,* rice-bran-and-salt pickled), 蘿蔔乾 (dried), Korean *동치미 (dongchimi,* winter radish water kimchi) and *깍두기 (kkakdugi,* cubed radish kimchi), and the long-simmered forms *おでん (oden)* and *風呂吹き大根 (furofuki daikon)*.

*The classical maxim, and it is counterintuitive until the mechanism is stated:* *冬吃蘿蔔夏吃薑* — "eat radish in winter, ginger in summer." *The obvious reading is wrong*: it is not matching a cooling food to the cold season. The classical argument runs by *interior-exterior inversion*. In winter yang is *stored internally*, people eat richly and move little, and *內熱* and *食積* — interior heat and food accumulation — are the predictable results; the radish *descends qi and disperses stagnation* and clears exactly that. In summer yang is dispersed to the *exterior* and the interior is relatively cold and depleted — the *暑傷氣* doctrine already set out in this book (see `zz-glossary.typ` and `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`) — so the warming drug is the apt one. *The maxim is about where the body's heat is, not about what the weather is doing*, and it is the same reasoning that underwrites the Korean custom of ginseng-chicken soup on the hottest days of the year (以熱治熱). *Cares:* the *cooked, simmered* form for the winter-interior-heat-and-stagnation indication; the *raw grated* form (大根おろし, daikon oroshi) reserved for *accompanying fried and fatty food and oily fish*, where its function is immediate and digestive; 紅葉おろし (momiji oroshi, grated with chilli) where a warming correction is wanted. In 中醫 terms the external excess of the zone is 寒 with 燥 in the north; the organ loaded is the Spleen, by overeating rather than by damp.

*Warm-dry Mediterranean and Middle Eastern — the Greco-Roman and Tibb zone.* Hot dry summers, mild wet winters; *Pitta*-aggravating and *ṣafrāwī*, with external 燥 + 熱 and Lung and Liver loaded (see `climates.typ`). *The radish here is a raw, salted, oil-and-vinegar item taken with a meal* — and every element of that is a classical corrective rather than a culinary accident: *salt and vinegar are the named muṣliḥ*, the oil moderates the dryness, and *the with-or-after-food position is the canonical instruction*. *A Hot-Dry drug in a hot-dry climate requires correction*, and the tradition supplies it; the uncorrected form — raw radish alone, before food — is the one the sources specifically warn against.

*Hot-humid monsoonal South Asia — the Ayurvedic zone.* Pitta-Kapha with the load in *varṣā* and *śarad*; external 濕熱 and the Spleen loaded. *The classical bāla / vṛddha mūlaka rule has a straightforwardly climatic reading*, and this is this book's inference rather than a classical statement: the *tender* root is *laghu* and suits a damp-heat climate where heaviness is the standing danger, while the *mature* root is *guru* and *abhiṣyandi* — channel-clogging — in a climate whose characteristic pathology is already obstruction of the channels by damp. *Cares:* the carminative correctives *hiṅgu* and *ajwain* in every traditional preparation (see `substances/asafoetida.typ`); cooking rather than raw consumption; the tender root preferred; and the winter-and-cool-season timing, since mūlī is a cool-season crop in India too and the hot-season root is both tougher and more sharply pungent.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Two mismatches are worth naming. *First, the form:* daikon reaches Western markets chiefly as a *raw salad and garnish* vegetable, which in a *cool-damp Atlantic* climate — Vāta-Kapha, external 寒濕, Spleen-yang loaded — is the wrong form of a substance whose cooked and pickled preparations are the ones that zone's constitution would want. Raw grated daikon is not harmful there, but it answers nothing the climate asks, and the *simmered* form would. *Second, and more interesting, the season:* year-round supermarket availability has erased the *winter crop* on which the 冬吃蘿蔔 maxim depends. *The maxim is not a statement about the vegetable but about a conjunction of the vegetable with a season and a way of eating* — stored root, rich winter diet, little movement, interior heat — and eating daikon in July because it is on the shelf is a clean *kāla-viruddha*, the right substance at the wrong time. *The Japanese raw-oroshi-with-fried-food pairing, by contrast, is a saṃyoga that travels well*: its logic is the immediate one of a pungent enzymatic raw vegetable against fat, and it holds in any climate.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Composition (raw root, per 100 g, indicative):* ~18 kcal; ~95 g water; ~4 g carbohydrate of which ~1.6 g fibre; ~0.6 g protein; negligible fat. *Vitamin C ~22 mg* (a meaningful figure for a vegetable eaten raw in quantity); potassium ~230 mg; folate, and small amounts of calcium and magnesium. *Nutritionally it is mostly water*, and the root's interest is pharmacological rather than nutritional.

*Two compositional points that matter more than the macros:*
- *The leaves are far richer than the root and are usually discarded.* Daikon tops carry substantially more *carotene, vitamin C, calcium and iron* than the root, and are a genuine leafy green. *The Chinese tradition treats 蘿蔔纓 as a drug in its own right* — a case where classical practice and modern nutrition agree against modern marketing, which sells the root trimmed.
- *Drying concentrates it dramatically.* *切り干し大根 (kiriboshi daikon)* is, per 100 g, many times higher in fibre, calcium, iron and potassium than the fresh root, the water having been removed and the sugars concentrated. *The storage preparation is also a nutritional upgrade*, which is the kind of thing a preservation technology is not usually credited with.

*Glucosinolates and the pungency mechanism — and it validates the classical drug-form distinction with unusual precision.* Daikon's characteristic pungent principle is *4-methylthio-3-butenyl isothiocyanate (MTBITC)*, generated from the glucosinolate *glucoraphasatin (dehydroerucin)* when *myrosinase* meets its substrate on *cell rupture*. Three consequences follow, and all three are visible in traditional practice:
- *The pungency requires physical damage* — hence *grating* (大根おろし) as a distinct preparation rather than a cutting style.
- *It is volatile and transient*, declining markedly within roughly half an hour of grating, which is why grated daikon is made to order.
- *Heat denatures myrosinase*, so *cooked daikon generates no isothiocyanate pungency at all* and is a sweet, mild, different food.
*This is a real convergence with the classical 生食升氣，熟食降氣 doctrine, and it should be stated carefully.* The enzymology explains why raw and cooked daikon are *different substances* with different sensory and chemical profiles — which is the doctrine's underlying claim. *It does not validate the specific directional language*: "raises qi" and "descends qi" are claims within a system the chemistry does not translate, and the honest statement is that the tradition was right that processing creates two drugs here, by a mechanism it could not have known, and that the particular directions it assigned are not thereby confirmed.

*Digestive enzymes — real, and routinely overstated.* Raw daikon contains *amylase (diastase)* and other hydrolytic enzymes, which is the usual explanation offered for the custom of serving grated daikon with fried food, grilled fish and rice. *The enzymes are genuinely present; their contribution to human digestion is modest*, since dietary enzymes are substantially denatured by gastric acid, and the culinary pairing is probably doing more by cutting richness and stimulating secretion than by direct enzymatic action. *Reported as a real constituent with an overstated mechanism.*

*Other pharmacology, honestly tiered:*
- *Isothiocyanates and chemoprevention* — the Brassicaceae isothiocyanates induce Phase II detoxification enzymes and show antiproliferative activity in cell and animal models. *Preclinical; no human outcome data for daikon specifically*, and the inference from a vegetable class to a vegetable to a clinical endpoint is exactly the step this book declines to take.
- *Black radish juice as a choleretic* — the modern survival of the Avicennan liver indication, with a modest European clinical and traditional-use literature for dyspepsia and biliary sluggishness. *Traditional-use registration rather than an evidence-based indication*; the convergence with the classical *mufattiḥ* attribution is the interesting part, not the strength of the trial data.
- *萊菔子 (seed)* — the Chinese-language literature reports prokinetic, antihypertensive and lipid effects, largely in animal models and small clinical studies. *Thin, and not independently replicated*; the seed's standing rests on classical use, not on this material.
- *Nitrates* — present as in other root and leaf vegetables, with the generally favourable vascular pharmacology of dietary nitrate; the contribution of daikon specifically is unstudied.
- *Goitrin* — the thyroid-relevant glucosinolate derivative; see Viruddha.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit.* *As a whole food there is no toxicological ceiling*, and the meaningful figures are practical rather than safety thresholds: *the limiting considerations are gastrointestinal (wind and bloating at high raw intake, relevant in IBS) and thyroidal (goitrin at high habitual raw intake combined with iodine deficiency)*, both mitigated by cooking. Ordinary culinary quantities — including the substantial portions normal in Japanese, Korean and Chinese eating — raise no safety question.

*As a medicinal the seed does have a dose figure*, and it should be kept distinct from the food: *萊菔子 5–12 g decocted* is the standard reference range, with the *limiting consideration being its dispersing-descending action rather than toxicity* — *氣虛者慎用*, use cautiously in qi vacuity, and avoid alongside qi-tonifying prescriptions and in pregnancy. *Reference figures requiring current verification against pharmacopoeial and clinical sources, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Composition figures: USDA FoodData Central for raw daikon and for radish leaves, and Japanese _Standard Tables of Food Composition_ for 切り干し大根, whose concentration effect is documented there — all figures indicative and requiring current verification. MTBITC, glucoraphasatin and the myrosinase mechanism, including the volatility and heat-lability: food-chemistry literature on _Raphanus_ glucosinolates. The reading of the enzymology as converging with 生食升氣，熟食降氣 only at the level of "processing creates two drugs," and not as validating the directional claims, is this book's framing and is flagged as such. Dietary-enzyme denaturation by gastric acid is standard physiology; the overstatement of the amylase mechanism is widespread in popular writing. Isothiocyanate chemoprevention: preclinical literature, with no daikon-specific human outcome data. Black radish choleretic preparations: European traditional-use registrations and the modest associated literature. 萊菔子 dose range and the 氣虛者慎用 caution: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_, Semen Raphani. Goitrin and thyroid iodine organification: standard nutrition and endocrinology literature; clinically relevant principally with concurrent iodine deficiency.]

